---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="Database" description="The point observations behind the peatland carbon maps: sources, the DNR Peat Inventory, gap-fill quality tiers, and carbon stock.">
	<h1>Database</h1>
	<p>
		The database contains the point data that was consolidated to build the prediction models.
		Independent field data collected in the summer of 2025 was used as a validation dataset.
	</p>

	<section class="db-section">
		<h2>Training data</h2>
		<p>
			Three sources were combined to be used as point data. The Minnesota DNR Peat Inventory, the
			USDA National Soil Information System (NASIS) pedon data, and Minnesota Biological Survey
			(MBS) relevé data. Point datasets were filtered depending on the property being modeled. More
			information on the point data can be found in the <a href="https://mn-lowland-conifers.github.io/03-peatland-carbon-modeling/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">MN Peatland Carbon Modeling E-book</a>.
		</p>
		<div class="table-wrap">
			<table>
				<thead>
					<tr><th>Dataset</th><th>Target</th><th class="num">Points</th></tr>
				</thead>
				<tbody>
					<tr><td>Probability</td><td><code>peat_binary</code> (peat present / absent)</td><td class="num">57,134</td></tr>
					<tr><td>Depth</td><td><code>depb</code> (cm)</td><td class="num">8,223</td></tr>
					<tr><td>Composition</td><td>Fibric, hemic and sapric percent</td><td class="num">18,041</td></tr>
				</tbody>
			</table>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="db-section">
		<h2>MN DNR Peat Inventory</h2>
		<p>
			Peat Inventory tables were merged on site ID into one horizon-level table so all lab
			measurements (ash, carbon, bulk density, and classification) are together. This resulted in
			35,142 horizons from 7,032 pedons. Missing top or bottom depths were filled from adjacent
			horizons where possible. Horizons that could not be corrected are extended to the next
			horizon or to a maximum depth of 200 cm.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="db-section">
		<h2>Filling the gaps</h2>
		<p>Soil organic carbon (SOC) and bulk density (Db) were then gap filled using the best method available based on existing data:</p>
		<ol>
			<li>Measured lab data: used when available (SOC: 533 horizons, BD: 3,209 horizons).</li>
			<li>
				Ash regression: with horizons that were missing SOC or BD but had ash content, a linear regression
				(SOC-Ash and BD-Ash) was fit and used to predict the missing value (SOC-Ash R²=0.623; BD-Ash
				R²=0.579).
			</li>
			<li>
				Random Forest: a random forest model was trained on von Post decomposition class, top/mid horizon
				depth, and horizon classification (Fibric, Hemic, Sapric). This performed poorly (SOC CV
				R²=−0.051).
			</li>
			<li>
				Still missing: horizons with no von Post score are left unfilled and excluded from carbon
				stock calculation
			</li>
		</ol>
		<div class="chart" data-chart="gap"></div>
		<div class="chart-pair">
			<div class="chart chart-half" data-chart="bd-ash"></div>
			<div class="chart chart-half" data-chart="soc-ash"></div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="db-section">
		<h2>Pedon eligibility</h2>
		<p>A pedon qualifies for use in the carbon stock calculation if it meets either of 2 criteria:</p>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<strong>Criteria A</strong>: observed peat depth of ≥ 100 cm, with complete SOC% and bulk density
				on all organic horizons in the profile.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Criteria B</strong>: a definitive bottom of organic material is observed (mineral or
				limnic layer underlying organics) which means the full peat profile was captured, and complete
				SOC% and bulk density on all organic horizons.
			</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			3,828 pedons meet one of these two criteria and make up the data set for carbon stock estimation.
			Of these, 1,609 meet Criterion B specifically (a definitive bottom of organic) which is the subset
			data for the full-profile carbon stock model.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="db-section">
		<h2>Quality tiers</h2>
		<p>
			After gap filling, the filled values were flagged by the method used to track data quality:
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li><strong style="color:#1B9E77">Tier 1</strong>: no RF-filled values used (measured or ash-regression only, 503 pedons, 13.1%)</li>
			<li><strong style="color:#D95F02">Tier 2</strong>: some RF-filled values (under 50% of horizons, 202 pedons, 5.3%)</li>
			<li><strong style="color:#7570B3">Tier 3</strong>: majority RF-filled (over 50% of horizons, 3,123 pedons, 81.6%)</li>
		</ul>
		<div class="chart" data-chart="tiers"></div>
		<div class="chart" data-chart="tier-box"></div>
		<p>
			For each eligible pedon carbon stock is calculated by horizon by multiplying the depth increment ×
			bulk density × SOC fraction, and then summed across all organic horizons in the profile.
		</p>
		<div class="chart chart-tall" data-chart="scatter"></div>
		<div class="chart" data-chart="histogram"></div>
	</section>

	<section class="db-section">
		<h2>Lab properties by peat type</h2>
		<p>
			Measured lab values from the Peat Inventory, grouped by peat classification or von Post
			humification class.
		</p>
		<div class="chart" data-chart="lab-box"></div>
	</section>

	<script>
		import '../../scripts/dbCharts';
	</script>
</Layout>

<style>
	.db-section {
		padding: 1.5rem 0 1rem;
	}

	.db-section > *:last-child {
		margin-bottom: 0;
	}

	.db-section h2 {
		margin: 0 0 0.55rem;
		font-size: clamp(1.45rem, 2.4vw, 1.65rem);
	}

	.db-section p {
		margin: 0 0 0.9rem;
	}

	.db-section ul,
	.db-section ol {
		margin: 0 0 1rem;
		padding-left: 1.5rem;
		line-height: 1.6;
	}

	.db-section li {
		margin-bottom: 0.4rem;
		color: var(--muted);
	}

	.table-wrap {
		overflow-x: auto;
		margin: 0.5rem 0 1rem;
	}

	table {
		border-collapse: collapse;
		width: 100%;
		font-size: 0.95rem;
	}

	th,
	td {
		text-align: left;
		padding: 0.5rem 0.75rem;
		border-bottom: 1px solid #cccccc;
	}

	th {
		background: #f1f1f1;
		font-weight: 600;
	}

	.num {
		text-align: right;
		font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
	}

	code {
		font-size: 0.88em;
		background: #f1f1f1;
		padding: 0.05rem 0.3rem;
		border-radius: 2px;
	}

	.chart {
		width: 100%;
		height: 460px;
		margin: 0.25rem 0 1.25rem;
	}

	.chart-tall {
		height: 540px;
	}

	.chart-pair {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
		gap: 1rem;
	}

	.chart-pair .chart-half {
		height: 440px;
	}

	.chart-error {
		display: grid;
		place-items: center;
		color: #777777;
		background: #ebebeb;
	}

	/* toggle buttons are created by the chart script */
	:global(.chart-toggle) {
		display: inline-flex;
		margin: 0.5rem 0.75rem 0.25rem 0;
		border: 1px solid #454545;
		border-radius: 4px;
		overflow: hidden;
	}

	:global(.chart-toggle-row) {
		display: flex;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
	}

	:global(.chart-toggle button) {
		font: inherit;
		font-size: 0.88rem;
		padding: 0.38rem 0.8rem;
		border: 0;
		border-right: 1px solid #454545;
		background: #ffffff;
		color: #272727;
		cursor: pointer;
	}

	:global(.chart-toggle button:last-child) {
		border-right: 0;
	}

	:global(.chart-toggle button[aria-pressed='true']) {
		background: #333333;
		color: #ffffff;
	}

	:global(.chart-toggle button:focus-visible) {
		outline: 2px solid #36899c;
		outline-offset: -2px;
	}

	@media (width <= 760px) {
		.chart-pair {
			grid-template-columns: 1fr;
		}

		.chart,
		.chart-tall {
			height: 400px;
		}
	}
</style>
